* text=auto

*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.ifc text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.tmdl text eol=lf
*.pbip text eol=lf
*.pbir text eol=lf
*.pbism text eol=lf
**/.platform text eol=lf

# The generated IDS reports under reports/ids/ are the only HTML here, and the
# checker already writes them with explicit LF on every platform. This line is
# therefore not a fix — it is a declaration. Without it the guarantee rests on
# an implementation detail of one exporter, and an editor, a formatter, or a
# future writer that used the platform default would put CRLF into the blob on
# Windows and quietly break the byte comparison that CI runs on Linux. The
# other generated text formats are pinned for the same reason; HTML was simply
# missed.
*.html text eol=lf

third_party/buildingsmart/bcf-xml/3.0/** -text
third_party/speckle/powerbi-visual/2026.6.0/LICENSE -text

# Legacy compatibility for data contract 0.1 only.
#
# The published v1.0.0 run_id is a SHA-256 over this file's raw bytes, taken
# from a CRLF working copy. Left to `text=auto` the file checks out with
# platform line endings, so the same commit yields a different run_id on Linux
# than on Windows — and run_id feeds every finding key. Pinning the bytes keeps
# the published baseline reproducible everywhere.
#
# This is a compatibility measure, not a statement about rule identity. Raw
# text bytes are not the semantic identity of a rule set: reindenting a
# document must not re-key a validation. The canonical design separates the
# two — `source_blob_sha256` records which exact file was read, while the
# normalized ruleset digest carries rule semantics and is what the new
# validation_run_id derives from. This line retires with the legacy adapters.
ids/*.ids -text
